    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d1171">

                <pb facs="0963" n="847" sameAs="frus:frus1912/pg847"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 812.00/5001.</note>
                <head ana="#head_untitled-inferred-from-preceding-sibling-head">[Untitled]</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">American Embassy</hi>, <lb/>
                            <hi rendition="#italic">Mexico</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1912-09-20T11:00:00-06:36">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">September 20, 1912—11 a.m.</hi>
                        </date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>The importance of either retaining Pasqual Orozco, sr., on American soil or
                    returning him to Mexico at a point held by revolutionists is a matter I wish to
                    bring urgently before the Department. Ernesto Madero says it had been arranged
                    for Governor Gonzalez, of Chihuahua, * * *<anchor n="1" xml:id="d1171-f1"/> and
                    that he will be immediately shot. Gonzalez is implacably anti-American, a menace
                    to American interests, who should be treated with small courtesy. The surrender
                    of Orozco to the Federal Government’s man under any guise or pretext would work
                    incalculable harm to defenseless Americans and American interests in districts
                    held by the revolutionary forces.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Wilson.</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
